function pz_hero_quickanswer_cat_indoor_enrichment_guide() { ?>
    <div class="pz-hero-quickanswer"><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Indoor cats need regular opportunities to climb, scratch, hunt-simulate through play, and explore to stay mentally healthy, since a lack of stimulation is linked to obesity, destructive behavior, and stress-related illness. Daily interactive play, vertical space like cat trees or shelves, puzzle feeders, and window access to the outside world are core pieces of a good enrichment routine. Signs of under-stimulation include over-grooming, excessive meowing, and destructive scratching.</div>
<?php }

function pz_what_is_cat_indoor_enrichment_guide() {
    ob_start(); ?>
    <p>Environmental enrichment means providing an indoor cat with opportunities to express natural behaviors, hunting, climbing, scratching, exploring, and resting in safe, elevated spots, that they would otherwise engage in outdoors. Because indoor cats don't have a changing landscape of prey, territory, and novelty to respond to, owners need to intentionally build that stimulation into the home.</p>
    <p>The Ohio State University Indoor Pet Initiative and the American Association of Feline Practitioners' environmental needs guidelines both describe five core feline needs: a safe space, multiple separated key resources (food, water, litter, resting areas), opportunity for play and predatory behavior, positive social interaction on the cat's terms, and an environment that respects a cat's sense of smell.</p>
    <p>When these needs aren't met, cats commonly develop behavioral or even physical problems, including obesity from inactivity, destructive scratching or furniture damage, excessive vocalization, and stress-related conditions like idiopathic cystitis or over-grooming.</p>
    <?php return ob_get_clean();
}

function pz_why_important_cat_indoor_enrichment_guide() {
    ob_start(); ?>
    <p>Enrichment isn't a luxury for indoor cats, it's a core part of their physical and emotional health, directly affecting weight, behavior, and stress levels.</p>
    <div class="pz-why-grid">
      <div class="pz-why-item">
        <span class="pz-why-icon">⚖️</span>
        <div><strong>Inactivity contributes to obesity</strong><p>Indoor cats without regular play and activity burn fewer calories, and obesity is one of the most common and preventable health problems in indoor cats.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="pz-why-item">
        <span class="pz-why-icon">🪑</span>
        <div><strong>Boredom drives destructive behavior</strong><p>Scratching furniture, knocking things over, and excessive meowing are frequently signs of an understimulated cat seeking an outlet, not simple misbehavior.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="pz-why-item">
        <span class="pz-why-icon">🧠</span>
        <div><strong>Mental stimulation matters as much as physical exercise</strong><p>Puzzle feeders and novel play help satisfy a cat\'s natural problem-solving and hunting instincts, which a static indoor environment doesn\'t provide on its own.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="pz-why-item">
        <span class="pz-why-icon">🩺</span>
        <div><strong>Chronic under-stimulation is linked to stress-related illness</strong><p>Conditions like feline idiopathic cystitis and psychogenic over-grooming are associated with environmental stress, which good enrichment can help reduce.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <?php return ob_get_clean();
}

